	abstract = {This investigation aimed to report the prevalence and its correlates of interpersonal physical violence (physical attacks and fighting) among adolescents in Bhutan. Cross-sectional nationally representative data were analysed from 7,576 adolescents (16 years median age) that participated in the “2016 Bhutan Global School-Based Student Health Survey (GSHS).” Results indicate that almost two in five participants (38.3%) reported physical attack (14.1% 1 time and 24.2% ≥2 times) in the past 12 months and 37.8% (17.9% 1 time and 21.0% ≥2 times) in physical fight (past 12 months). In adjusted multinomial logistic regression analysis, male sex, younger age, rural residence, ever drunk, current tobacco use, ever sex, attending physical education, soft drink intake, passive smoking, psychological distress, school truancy and low parental support were associated with 1 time and/or 2 or more times having been physically attacked in the past 12 months. Male sex, younger age, rural residence, ever drunk, current tobacco use, current cannabis use, ever sex, attending physical education, soft drink intake, passive smoking, school truancy, psychological distress, and low parental and peer support were associated with 1 time and/or 2 or more times having been in a physical fight. Almost two in five of the students were involved in interpersonal violence (physical attacks and physical fighting) and several associated variables were detected which can aid in designing intervention strategies. © Nova Science Publishers, Inc.},
